"Don’t Retrain, Remix: Adaptation of Web Agents using Mixture Hypernetworks (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1)\nciteturn41257view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1\",\"lineno\":130}); Total lines: 600\nL117: Along the temporal axis, TimeWarp evaluates agents on successive eras of web design (cite69†Ishmam and Marino, 2026 ). Complementary diagnostic suites isolate individual factors by perturbing the layout, interaction semantics, visual attributes, or network events of otherwise fixed tasks (cite90†Bai et al., 2026 ; cite91†Kara et al., 2025 ). On all of these benchmarks, agents lose accuracy on interfaces they were not trained on.\nL118: Mind2Web reports substantially lower performance for fine-tuned agents on unseen websites and domains than on its cross-task split (cite68†Deng et al., 2023 ), and TimeWarp finds that text-based agents become brittle under interface changes after fine-tuning on a single version (cite69†Ishmam and Marino, 2026 ). A practical web agent should therefore adapt at test time to visually and functionally unfamiliar interfaces without relying on extensive demonstrations. We evaluate WebMix along both axes.\nL119: TimeWarp provides both domain shifts and version shifts, and WebArena-Lite-v2 (cite92†Liu et al., 2024 ; cite93†Liu et al., 2026 ) provides domain shifts.\nL120: ##### Adapting Web Agents to Unseen Interfaces.\nL121: An agent facing an unfamiliar interface must learn something about it before it can act well. Prior work differs in where that knowledge comes from and where it is kept. The most direct source is a human: AdaptAgent adapts multimodal agents to new websites from up to two human demonstrations, using in-context demonstrations for proprietary models and meta-learned adaptation for open-weight models (cite80†Verma et al., 2025 ).\nL122: Demonstrations, however, are costly to collect, so a second line of work lets the agent gather its own experience through guided or unguided exploration (cite81†Murty et al., 2024 ; cite94†Su et al., 2025 ; cite95†Pahuja et al., 2025 ; cite96†Sun et al., 2024 ). Beyond demonstrations and raw experience, an agent can also carry forward explicit knowledge.\nL123: Non-parametric methods keep the weights fixed and store what the agent learns outside them: as reusable workflows (cite82†Wang et al., 2024 ) or episodic memories (cite97†Sarch et al., 2025 ), as an instruction manual written through interaction (cite98†Chen et al., 2024 ), as skills distilled from practice (cite99†Zheng et al., 2025 ; cite100†Wang et al., 2026b ), or as summaries of environment dynamics learned at deployment time (cite83†Chen et al., 2025 ).\nL124: Like AutoManual (cite98†Chen et al., 2024 ), WebMix constructs an explicit description of the environment from interaction. AutoManual uses this description to condition planning, whereas we use it to predict a composition of existing adapters. More generally, prior methods treat each new interface as a separate adaptation problem. WebMix instead learns an interface-conditioned rule for composing adapters on the source interfaces and applies it unchanged to the target interface.\nL125: Target-side exploration supplies the conditioning information, without target demonstrations or test-time gradient updates.\nL126: ##### Adapter Composition and Generation.\nL127: Low-rank adaptation (cite79†Hu et al., 2022 ) lets many adapters share one frozen base model. A growing body of work reuses such collections instead of training a new model for each task (cite101†Yadav et al., 2024 ). The simplest form of reuse combines adapters in weight space. Merging methods add or average updates arithmetically (cite102†Ilharco et al., 2023 ; cite103†Yadav et al., 2023 ; cite104†Wortsman et al., 2022 ).\nL128: Other approaches optimize composition coefficients on target examples, as in LoraHub (cite105†Huang et al., 2024 ), or compose adapters dynamically during inference through layer-wise gating in MoLE (cite106†Wu et al., 2024 ) and token-level routing in Arrow and PHATGOOSE (cite107†Ostapenko et al., 2024 ; cite108†Muqeeth et al., 2024 ).\nL129: A more ambitious alternative skips the bank altogether and generates the adapter with a hypernetwork (cite84†Ha et al., 2017 ), conditioned on contexts and documents (cite109†Charakorn et al., 2026 ), task descriptions and prompts (cite85†Charakorn et al., 2025 ; cite110†Liang et al., 2025 ), whole code repositories (cite111†Hotsko et al., 2026 ), or per-sample memories in continual learning (cite112†Wang et al., 2026a ). Generation is flexible, but it is hard to learn.\nL130: The generator must emit every entry of every LoRA factor, and because LoRA factors are not identifiable, many different factors encode the same update. This is manageable with abundant conditionings but not with few: Drag-and-Drop LLMs, for example, improve little over their training adapters when trained on only a few datasets (cite110†Liang et al., 2025 ), and a family of web interfaces provides only a handful. WebMix sits between these two lines.\nL131: Like a hypernetwork, it maps a description of the target to an adapter in one forward pass. Like merging, it builds that adapter from a frozen bank. It predicts only one coefficient per bank adapter and emits a single standard LoRA adapter that an unmodified inference stack can serve. We compare against Text-to-LoRA conditioned on the same interface representation, following cite111†Hotsko et al. (2026) , and analyze an idealization of it in the Appendix §cite56†D.2 .\nL132: cite113†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Overview of WebMix. A scout explores the target website. Its Explorer interacts with the environment, and its Writer turns the observations into a compact manual $m$. An encoder maps $m$ to an embedding $h$, from which a router predicts mixing weights $c_{1},\\dots,c_{5}$ over a frozen bank of KL-anchored LoRA adapters.\nL133: Their weighted sum is a site-specific adapter $\\Delta W$, added to the base weights $W_{\\theta}$ of the agent $\\pi_{\\theta}$, which reads the accessibility tree and emits grounded actions (e.g., click [31]). Websites vary across versions ($v_{1}$–$v_{5}$) and domains.\nL134: ## 3 WebMix: Adaptation through Composition\nL135: \nL136: Figure cite114†2 gives an overview. §cite12†3.1 states the problem, §cite16†3.2 describes the mixture hypernetwork, and §cite21†3.3 describes its training and its adaptation to an unseen interface.\nL137: ### 3.1 Preliminaries\nL138: A web agent is a language model that completes a task on a website through a browser. It reads a natural-language goal and the current page, and it emits one browser action at a time, such as a click, a typed string, or a scroll, until the task ends. A task succeeds if a verifier accepts the final state of the website. A web environment consists of several interfaces. Each interface is either a website domain, such as a wiki or an online shop, or a version, i.e., a design of the website.\nL139: Interfaces differ in presentation, navigation structure, and content. Web agents are often trained using parameter-efficient methods, such as Low Rank Adaptation (LoRA) (cite79†Hu et al., 2022 ). We now formalize the web environment, agents, LoRA, and our problem setting in the following paragraphs.\nL140: ##### Web environment and agents.\nL141: We model the agent’s interaction with a web interface as a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP) $\\langle\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{A},\\mathcal{O},\\mathbb{T},\\mathbb{T}_{0},\\mathbb{O},R_{g}\\rangle$ (cite115†Kaelbling et al., 1998 ). A state $s\\in\\mathcal{S}$ comprises the website’s front-end (external) and back-end (internal) states. An episode starts from $s_{0}\\sim\\mathbb{T}_{0}$, and browser actions $a\\in\\mathcal{A}$ change the state according to $s_{t+1}\\sim\\mathbb{T}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t},a_{t})$.\nL142: The agent receives only an observation $o_{t}\\sim\\mathbb{O}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t})\\in\\mathcal{O}$, in the form of an accessibility tree (cite87†Zhou et al., 2024 ). A task has a natural-language goal $g$ and a verifier $V_{g}:\\mathcal{S}\\rightarrow\\{0,1\\}$, which defines the terminal reward $R_{g}(s_{T})=V_{g}(s_{T})$ at the final step $T$.\nL143: The agent is a policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$ with parameters $\\theta$ that selects each action from the interaction history, $a_{t}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid H_{t})$, where $H_{t}=(g,o_{0:t},a_{0:t-1})$. Let $\\mathcal{E}$ be the set of interfaces of the environment. We index the source interfaces as $\\mathcal{E}_{\\mathrm{src}}=\\{v_{1},\\dots,v_{K}\\}\\subset\\mathcal{E}$, so $K=|\\mathcal{E}_{\\mathrm{src}}|$, and write an unseen interface as $u\\in\\mathcal{E}\\setminus\\mathcal{E}_{\\mathrm{src}}$.\nL144: Each source interface $v$ has a demonstration corpus $\\mathcal{D}_{v}=\\{(x,y)\\}$, and we write $\\mathcal{D}_{k}=\\mathcal{D}_{v_{k}}$. Each input $x$ is a history $H_{t}$, rendered as a prompt that contains the goal, the accessibility tree of the current page, and a record of the earlier steps. Each target $y=(y_{1},\\dots,y_{|y|})$ is the expert’s response, a token sequence that contains its reasoning and one browser action.\nL145: ##### Low-rank adaptation (LoRA).\nL146: \nL147: Fine-tuning all of $\\theta$ for each interface would store one copy of the model per interface. Low-rank adaptation (LoRA) (cite79†Hu et al., 2022 ) keeps $\\theta$ frozen and learns a low-rank update to selected weight matrices. Let $\\Omega$ index the adapted weight matrices layer-wise, and let $W_{\\theta,\\ell}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{out}}\\times d_{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{in}}}$ be the frozen matrix at $\\ell\\in\\Omega$. LoRA replaces it with\nL148:  | $$W_{\\ell}=W_{\\theta,\\ell}+\\Delta W_{\\ell},\\qquad\\Delta W_{\\ell}=\\frac{\\alpha}{r}\\,B_{\\ell}A_{\\ell},$$  |  | (1)\nL149: where $A_{\\ell}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{in}}}$ and $B_{\\ell}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{out}}\\times r}$ are trainable factors, $r$ is the adaptation rank, and the scale $\\alpha$ sets the size of the update independently of $r$. $A_{\\ell}$ is initialized randomly and $B_{\\ell}$ is set to zero, so training starts from the base policy.\nL150: We call the collection of updates $\\Delta\\theta=(\\Delta W_{\\ell})_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}$ an adapter, and write $\\pi_{\\theta+\\Delta\\theta}$ for the policy with the adapter installed. Adapters can be held separate from the base weights and applied at serving time, so many adapters can share one frozen base model. We adapt the attention projections (query, key, value, output) and the MLP projections (gate, up, down), with $r=16$ and $\\alpha=32$.\nL151: ##### Problem setting.\nL152: We start from a frozen base model and a set of source interfaces, each with a corpus of expert demonstrations. Our goal is for the agent to succeed on an unseen interface. In domain transfer, it is an unseen domain, and in version transfer, it is an unseen version. The unseen interface has no demonstrations, and no parameter is optimized on it. Before the agent starts its tasks, it may only make a brief, bounded visit to the unseen interface, which we call scouting.\nL153: We ask whether the parameters the agent needs on the unseen interface can be assembled from what it learned on the source interfaces, using this visit alone.\nL154: ### 3.2 The Mixture Hypernetwork\nL155: \nL156: A hypernetwork (cite84†Ha et al., 2017 ) is a network that outputs the parameters of another network. WebMix uses a hypernetwork $F_{\\phi}$ with trainable parameters $\\phi$ to produce the adapter for an interface from a bounded exploration record of it. For an unseen interface $u$ with record $E_{u}$, $F_{\\phi}$ outputs one update per adapted matrix, which is added to the frozen base weights,\nL157:  | $$\\{\\Delta W_{u,\\ell}\\}_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}=F_{\\phi}(E_{u}),\\qquad W_{u,\\ell}=W_{\\theta,\\ell}+\\Delta W_{u,\\ell}.$$  |  | (2)\nL158: The updates $\\Delta\\theta_{u}=(\\Delta W_{u,\\ell})_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}$ produce the adapted policy $\\pi_{\\theta+\\Delta\\theta_{u}}$, with all other parameters unchanged. The objective is to maximize task success on $u$ without target demonstrations or parameter optimization on the target interface.\nL159: $F_{\\phi}$ has three parts, described below: a frozen bank of adapters, a small router with the trainable parameters $\\phi$, and a frozen scout that maps the record $E_{v}$ of an interface $v$ to a representation $h_{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}$.\nL160: ##### The adapter bank.\nL161: The bank holds one LoRA adapter per source interface. For each $v_{k}$, the bank contains factors $\\{(A^{(k)}_{\\ell},B^{(k)}_{\\ell})\\}_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}$ of rank $r$ and scale $\\alpha$, with updates $\\Delta W^{(k)}_{\\ell}=(\\alpha/r)\\,B^{(k)}_{\\ell}A^{(k)}_{\\ell}$. We write $\\Delta\\theta^{(k)}=(\\Delta W^{(k)}_{\\ell})_{\\ell\\in\\Omega}$ for adapter $k$, and $\\pi^{(k)}=\\pi_{\\theta+\\Delta\\theta^{(k)}}$ for the policy using it.\nL162: The bank $\\{\\Delta\\theta^{(1)},\\dots,\\Delta\\theta^{(K)}\\}$ is the source knowledge that WebMix transfers to an unseen interface. All bank adapters share the base model, the index set $\\Omega$, the rank $r$, and the scale $\\alpha$, so they can be combined matrix-wise. The bank is trained once (Stage 1, §cite22†3.3.1 ) and stays frozen afterwards.\nL163: ##### What the hypernetwork predicts.\nL164: \nL165: A direct hypernetwork, such as Text-to-LoRA (cite85†Charakorn et al., 2025 ), predicts every entry of every LoRA factor. The router of WebMix predicts only $K$ mixing coefficients, one per bank adapter. It is a two-layer perceptron with parameters $\\phi=(U_{1},b_{1},U_{2},b_{2})$ that maps $h_{v}$ to a vector of mixture weights over the $K$ bank adapters,\nL166:  | $$c_{v}=\\operatorname{softmax}\\!\\left(U_{2}\\,\\operatorname{SiLU}(U_{1}h_{v}+b_{1})+b_{2}\\right)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K},$$  |  | (3)\nL167: where $U_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\\times d_{c}}$, $b_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}}$, $U_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K\\times d_{h}}$, $b_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K}$, and $d_{h}$ is the hidden width ($d_{h}=256$, Appendix cite39†A.3 ). The coefficient $c_{v,k}$ is the weight of bank adapter $k$ in the adapter for $v$. A single coefficient vector is shared across all adapted matrices. We initialize $U_{2}$ and $b_{2}$ to zero, so the untrained router weights all bank adapters equally.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructured Authority Injection: Persistent Human Control for LLM Agents via Typed MCP Overrides (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22373v1)\nciteturn41257view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22373v1\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 298\nL71: Human-in-the-loop control has long been studied in shared autonomy, including mixed-initiative [cite42†14 ] and sliding autonomy frameworks [cite43†31 ], where control is dynamically shared between humans and robots. Language-based correction mechanisms such as LILAC enable natural-language feedback for manipulation, though without explicit protocol structure [cite44†6 ]. More recent LLM-based systems incorporate human feedback into multi-robot planning loops.\nL72: HMCF [cite45†20 ] and Sirius [cite46†22 ] integrate operator corrections into plan revision or policy updates, while BrainBody-LLM [cite47†4 ] uses closed-loop human signals to trigger replanning in embodied tasks. However, across these systems, human feedback is typically represented as unstructured language or control signals, lacking semantic parsing or protocol-level validation, which limits persistence and compositionality across planning steps.\nL73: ### II-C MCP in Robotic Systems\nL74: The Model Context Protocol (MCP) [cite28†3 ] provides a standardized interface between LLMs and external tools. In robotics, ROS-based MCP wrappers [cite30†9 , cite48†39 ] expose robot control and sensing interfaces as structured tools, while IoT-MCP [cite49†36 ] extends this paradigm to heterogeneous sensor networks.\nL75: MCP-Zero [cite50†8 ] further enables dynamic tool discovery with reduced token overhead, and recent work has also highlighted security and access-control challenges in MCP-enabled agentic systems [cite29†16 ].\nL76: ## III Structured Authority Injection\nL77: \nL78: cite51†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: The workflow and architecture of Structured Authority Injection (SAI): human supervisors submit schema-validated MCP override calls (Constraint, Priority, and Strategy), which are verified and persisted by the authority-context manager under deterministic precedence rules ($C\\succ P\\succ S$), then prepended to every subsequent LLM planning prompt to prevent authority erosion during multi-robot task allocation.\nL79: SAI is a protocol that requires no fine-tuning, no access to model weights, and no changes to the LLM’s tool vocabulary. It operates entirely at the interface between the human supervision layer and the LLM prompt (as shown in Figure cite52†1 ), exploiting two properties of instruction-tuned models that are already present: (i) the ability to follow typed tool schemas, and (ii) the tendency to attend more strongly to early context [cite34†24 , cite35†34 ].\nL80: The three components of SAI—the override taxonomy, the MCP tool schemas, and the persistence model—are interdependent and together constitute the protocol.\nL81: ### III-A SAI Taxonomy\nL82: A central design decision in SAI is to treat operator corrections not as an undifferentiated message stream, but as typed objects whose structure encodes the kind of authority being exercised. We identify three dimensions along which corrections differ fundamentally: (a) the epistemic source: what the operator knows that the LLM does not; (b) the affected scope: a single robot, a task class, or the whole fleet; and (c) the persistence horizon: how long the correction should constrain future planning.\nL83: Conflating these dimensions forces the LLM to infer all three from free text, introducing the ambiguity failure mode [cite32†32 ].\nL84: Analysis of failure events logged across our eight evaluation scenarios, combined with a review of the HRI supervisory-control literature [cite53†10 , cite43†31 ], yields three semantically distinct and mutually exclusive correction classes that together cover the full observed correction space.\nL85: Class C (Constraint). Encodes physical or operational limits absent from the world-state snapshot. The planning agent’s world model derives entirely from the JSON state serialized at each epoch; it has no access to out-of-band signals such as radio alerts, sensor failures, or direct operator observation. Constraint overrides inject this knowledge as hard or soft rules scoped to a robot, a task, or a spatial region:\nL86: Hard constraints must never be violated; soft constraints are bypassed only when no compliant assignment exists. Both subtypes persist until the operator explicitly lifts them.\nL87: \nL88: Class P (Priority). Encodes urgency information received through channels outside the simulation, such as field radio calls, wearable sensor readings, medical status updates [cite45†20 ]. A priority override promotes a single task or an entire task class to a new urgency level:\nL89: The scope parameter (e.g., this_job or job_type) determines whether the adjustment applies to one instance or to all tasks of that type. Priority overrides expire automatically when the affected task or class is marked complete, requiring no manual cancellation.\nL90: \nL91: Class S (Strategy). Encodes coordination heuristics that should govern robot behavior over a bounded horizon, including zone distribution, formation sequencing, concurrency limits [cite39†5 ]:\nL92: Strategy overrides are the most abstract class and carry a scope parameter (e.g., mission or next_N) that triggers automatic expiry after exactly $N$ planning cycles. This temporal scoping is a deliberate usability choice: operators under high cognitive load should not be required to remember to cancel time-limited directives [cite53†10 ].\nL93: The three classes impose a strict conflict-resolution order $\\text{C}\\succ\\text{P}\\succ\\text{S}$. Within a class, a later override supersedes the earlier one for the same robot or task identifier without canceling overrides on different identifiers. This precedence rule is evaluated deterministically at prompt construction time, adding no LLM inference to the planning loop.\nL94: ### III-B MCP Tool Schemas and Persistence Model\nL95: \nL96: The taxonomy in Section cite12†III-A defines what each correction class means; the MCP tool schemas and the persistence model define how corrections enter and persist in the agent’s planning context. These two mechanisms jointly eliminate the ambiguity and authority-erosion failure modes.\nL97: \nL98: Algorithm 1 SAI authority-context lifecycle (per epoch).\nL99: \nL100: 1:Record set $\\mathcal{A}$; override $o$ (if any); world-state $W$; history $H$\nL101: 2:Updated $\\mathcal{A}$; tool calls $C$\nL102: \nL103: 3:% Phase 1: override ingestion\nL104: \nL105: 4:if $o$ received from supervision interface then\nL106: \nL107: 5:  assert valid caller credential and JSON Schema $\\triangleright$ else return error; LLM never sees $o$\nL108: \nL109: 6:  $\\mathcal{A}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{A}\\cup\\{o\\}$\nL110: \nL111: 7:  Supersede earlier record in $\\mathcal{A}$ with same class and entity identifier as $o$\nL112: \nL113: 8:end if\nL114: \nL115: 9:% Phase 2: prompt construction and planning\nL116: \nL117: 10:Sort $\\mathcal{A}$: $\\text{C}\\succ\\text{P}\\succ\\text{S}$\nL118: 11:$P\\leftarrow[\\textsc{SysPrompt}]\\oplus\\textsc{Render}(\\mathcal{A})\\oplus[W]\\oplus[H]$\nL119: \nL120: 12:$C\\leftarrow\\textsc{LLM}(P)$\nL121: \nL122: 13:% Phase 3: record expiry\nL123: \nL124: 14:for all $a\\in\\mathcal{A}$ do\nL125: \nL126: 15:  if $a.\\textit{class}\\in\\{\\texttt{C},\\texttt{P}\\}$ and JobComplete($a$) then\nL127: \nL128: 16:    $\\mathcal{A}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{A}\\setminus\\{a\\}$ $\\triangleright$ job resolved or operator-cancelled\nL129: \nL130: 17:  else if $a.\\textit{class}=\\texttt{S}$ and $a.\\textit{scope}=\\texttt{next\\_N}$ then\nL131: \nL132: 18:    $a.r\\leftarrow a.r-1$\nL133: 19:    if $a.r=0$ then\nL134: \nL135: 20:     $\\mathcal{A}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{A}\\setminus\\{a\\}$ $\\triangleright$ auto-expire after $N$ cycles\nL136: \nL137: 21:    end if\nL138: \nL139: 22:  else\nL140: \nL141: 23:    skip $\\triangleright$ mission-scoped S: persists until end\nL142: \nL143: 24:  end if\nL144: \nL145: 25:end for\nL146: \nL147: 26:return $\\mathcal{A},\\,C$\nL148: Schema-validated tool calls. Each override class is exposed as a typed MCP tool [cite28†3 ]. The schemas enforce three design principles: (i) every parameter that affects persistence, scope, or precedence is explicit and typed—never inferred from free text; (ii) enumerations constrain all bounded value spaces (severity, scope, new_priority); (iii) free-text fields are restricted to human-readable rationale slots and carry no structural parameters.\nL149: Schema validation runs server-side before any record reaches the planner [cite29†16 ]. Invalid submissions, such as a missing robot_id, an out-of-range priority, or an ill-formed scope, are rejected immediately with a structured error returned to the supervision interface, prompting the operator to correct the input.\nL150: The LLM therefore never receives a malformed override record: the validation firewall is architecturally prior to the prompt pipeline, eliminating the ambiguity failure mode at submission time rather than at inference time.\nL151: Asymmetric callability. A role-checking middleware in the MCP server enforces that SAI override tools are callable only by the supervision interface, identified by a trusted caller credential attached to each request. The planning agent can observe active override records in its authority context but cannot create, modify, or delete them.\nL152: Persistent authority context. To eliminate authority erosion, SAI maintains a persistent authority context (an ordered list of active override records), that is prepended to every subsequent LLM prompt. Each record stores the override class, validated parameters, a one-sentence human-readable summary, a creation timestamp, and (for next_N overrides) a remaining-count $r\\in\\mathbb{N}$ decremented after each planning cycle.\nL153: The context is rendered as a numbered directive list and inserted at the very beginning of the system prompt, before the world-state snapshot and tool-call history. This placement exploits the primacy effect in instruction-tuned LLMs [cite35†34 , cite34†24 ]: early-context tokens receive disproportionately high attention weights. Together, positional primacy and explicit precedence instruction ensure that human directives structurally outrank model priors without fine-tuning or retrieval-augmented memory.\nL154: Records expire by one of three routes: constraint and priority overrides persist until the operator cancels them or job completion fires for the affected entity; mission-scoped strategy overrides persist until end of mission; next_N-scoped overrides are removed automatically when their remaining-count reaches zero. Algorithm cite54†1 formalizes the complete per-epoch lifecycle (override ingestion, prompt construction, agent planning, and record expiration) as a single coherent procedure.\nL155: ### III-C Theoretical Properties\nL156: \nL157: We state three properties of SAI that distinguish it from free-text override approaches and motivate its use as a general protocol.\nL158: \nL159: Property 1 (Submission-time validity). Every override record that enters the authority context has been validated against its class schema. The LLM never encounters a structurally incomplete or type-invalid directive.\nL160: Property 2 (Tamper resistance). The LLM cannot modify the authority context. Because SAI override tools are callable only by the supervision interface, a misbehaving or adversarially prompted model cannot inject, escalate, or remove directives [cite29†16 ]. This property holds as long as the MCP server correctly enforces caller credentials.\nL161: Property 3 (Deterministic conflict resolution). Conflicts between override records are resolved by static precedence, not by LLM inference. This means the outcome of a conflict is predictable and auditable without inspecting model internals. Combined with the full audit trail maintained by the logging middleware, SAI produces a complete, verifiable record of every authority decision made during a mission.\nL162: These three properties together address a gap in current LLM-agent deployment practice: the absence of a verifiable human authority layer that is architecturally prior to model inference [cite55†29 ]. SAI provides this layer without requiring model modification, making it applicable to any instruction-tuned model that supports MCP tool use [cite56†19 ].\nL163: TABLE I: Scenario details: eight planning failure modes.\nL164: #  | Failure mode  | Setup summary  | SAI class\nL165: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL166: S1  | Priority mis-order  | 2 robots; high-priority medical task far from spawn  | Priority\nL167: S2  | Zone distribution  | 3 robots clustered left; must spread across both zones  | Strategy\nL168: S3  | Compound  | 3 robots; satisfy priority + zone rules simultaneously  | P + S\nL169: S4  | Late interrupt  | SearchTask spawns at tick 10; redirect robot  | Priority\nL170: S5  | Unreachable task  | High-priority task in sealed room; must not assign  | Constraint\nL171: S6  | Capability mismatch  | 6 robots $\\times$ 3 types; 8 tasks with requirements  | Constraint\nL172: S7  | Bottleneck  | 6 robots; single-cell corridor; limit concurrency  | Strategy\nL173: S8  | Formation  | 5 robots; 2 cargo tasks need $\\geq$3 robots; sequence  | Strategy\nL174: ## IV Experimental Evaluation\nL175: \nL176: TABLE II: Task success rate comparison between baseline and SAI across eight scenarios.\nL177:  | S1  | S2  | S3  | S4  | S5  | S6  | S7  | S8\nL178: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL179: LLM  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI  | Base  | SAI\nL180: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL181: ASI1  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 0.00  | 0.00  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 0.88  | 1.00  | 0.86  | 1.00  | 0.00\nL182: Claude-Haiku  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.83  | 1.00  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.50\nL183: DeepSeek-R1  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 1.00  | 0.00  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | –  | 1.00  | 1.00\nL184: Gemini-2.0-Flash  | 0.50  | 0.50  | 1.00  | 0.50  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 1.00  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 0.88  | 0.43  | 0.57  | 1.00  | 0.50\nL185: Gemma-4-26B  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 1.00  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 1.00  | 0.88  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00\nL186: GPT-4o  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 0.83  | 0.75  | 0.75  | 0.88  | 0.88  | 1.00  | 0.57  | 1.00  | 1.00\nL187: GPT-OSS-20B  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.00  | 1.00  | 0.20  | 1.00  | 0.80  | 0.17  | 0.00  | 0.75  | 0.63  | 1.00  | 1.00  | 0.57  | 0.00  | 1.00\n"
